 \, working in the campus photography lab. Over the next 46 years\, he beca
 me a familiar presence throughout the college community\, documenting the 
 people\, programs and milestones that shaped generations of students and e
 mployees.</p><p>  The exhibition will trace Chown's evolution as a photogr
 apher\, from working with film and developing photographs in the darkroom 
 to embracing digital photography. It also offers a visual history of SRJC\
 , preserving moments from nearly five decades of college life.</p><p>  The
  exhibition will trace Chown's evolution as a photographer\, from working 
 with film and developing photographs in the darkroom to embracing digital 
 photography. It also offers a visual history of SRJC\, preserving moments 
 from nearly five decades of college life.</p><p>  The exhibition is free a
 nd open to the public.</p><p><strong>Location:</strong> Robert F. Agrella 
 Art Gallery\, Doyle Library 1st floor - Santa Rosa Campus</p><p><a href="h
