X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_livewhale
SUMMARY:Museum Reception -Standing in Her Power: Portraits Beyond the Colo
 nial Frame
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261114T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261114T180000
DTSTAMP:20260820T141610Z
UID:20261115T000000Z-23553@calendar.santarosa.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261114T160000
ATTACH:https://calendar.santarosa.edu/live/image/gid/3/width/80/height/80/
 crop/1/src_region/0,0,4830,4830/683_Standing_In_Her_Power_-_SRJC_Museum.re
 v.1787260045.png
CATEGORIES:Museum
DESCRIPTION:In early May 2026\, we put out a call for nominations\, asking
  for Native American women to represent their Nations\, their stories\, an
 d their strength through a powerful portrait exhibition celebrating identi
 ty\, cultural legacy\, and the resilience of Native women. We received far
  more nominations than we expected. Choosing from so many incredible women
  whose stories deserve to be told was not easy. Every single entry showed 
 us how beautiful and unique and strong Native American women are.  The ide
 a for the Standing in Her Power exhibit was conceived in the Fall of 2025 
 by student employee Julian Cook\, who wanted to showcase photos of Native 
 American women wearing regalia. Through Julian's connections in the Bay Ar
 ea\, the project has grown and led to an exciting collaboration with photo
 grapher Alexa Treviño. Cook continues to push the exhibit forward through
  deep conversations with the participants in order to add personal stateme
 nts about personal power and growth  to the photographs.This project is fu
 nded by a grant from the Santa Rosa Junior College Foundation Randolph New
 man Cultural Enrichment Endowment.For more information\, visit the SRJC Mu
 lticultural Museum website. (https://museum.santarosa.edu/event/standing-h
 er-power-portraits-beyond-colonial-frame)
LAST-MODIFIED:20260827T174756Z
LOCATION:Multicultural Museum - Santa Rosa Campus
URL:https://calendar.santarosa.edu/event/23553-museum-reception-standing-i
 n-her-power-portraits-beyon
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  In early May 2026\, we put out a call fo
 r nominations\, asking for Native American women to represent their Nation
 s\, their stories\, and their strength through a powerful portrait exhibit
 ion celebrating identity\, cultural legacy\, and the resilience of Native 
 women. We received far more nominations than we expected. Choosing from so
  many incredible women whose stories deserve to be told was not easy. Ever
 y single entry showed us how beautiful and unique and strong Native Americ
 an women are.</p><p>  The idea for the Standing in Her Power exhibit was c
 onceived in the Fall of 2025 by student employee Julian Cook\, who wanted 
 to showcase photos of Native American women wearing regalia. Through Julia
 n's connections in the Bay Area\, the project has grown and led to an exci
 ting collaboration with photographer Alexa Treviño. Cook continues to pus
 h the exhibit forward through deep conversations with the participants in 
 order to add personal statements about personal power and growth<br />  to
  the photographs.</p><p><em>This project is funded by a grant from the San
 ta Rosa Junior College Foundation Randolph Newman Cultural Enrichment Endo
 wment.</em></p><p><a href="https://museum.santarosa.edu/event/standing-her
 -power-portraits-beyond-colonial-frame" target="_blank" rel="noopener">For
  more information\, visit the SRJC Multicultural Museum website.</a></p>
